# Vision runs as a failover chain of real providers, tried in this order:
#   1. google-1  (GEMINI_API_KEY_1)  - Google Gemini, gemini-3.6-flash
#   2. google-2  (GEMINI_API_KEY_2)  - Google Gemini, gemini-3.6-flash
#   3. tabi      (TABI_AI_KEY)       - TabiAI, claude-opus-5
# Any tier error (rate limit 429, timeout, bad key) falls through to the next
# tier instantly. Without at least one key the backend refuses to start,
# because no scan could be answered and the app would only ever say
# "pehchan nahi ho saki".
# GEMINI_API_KEY_1=your_first_google_gemini_api_key
# GEMINI_API_KEY_2=your_second_google_gemini_api_key
# TABI_AI_KEY=your_tabiai_api_key

# TabiAI endpoint. Defaults to https://tabitoken.com/v1; set only if that changes.
# TABI_BASE_URL=https://tabitoken.com/v1

# Older deployments keep working with the legacy names GEMINI_API_KEY (tier 1)
# and TABI_API_KEY (TabiAI tier) when the names above are absent.

# There is no test-data mode and no synthetic fallback. A scan either returns
# what a real provider read, or it fails honestly.

# Optional, and only read by `python app.py` to switch Flask's debug reloader
# off. Production runs gunicorn (see Procfile and railway.json), so it has no
# effect on a Railway deployment.
# APP_MODE=production
